//? Title: ConvLSTM: Explicit Masking, State Paths and Source Controls
//? Subtitle: Native recurrent tensor components and an editable source training loop
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The original conv lstm experiment is retained with source fixtures, parameter initialization, full state histories and gradient controls. Reusable model math lives in datamunge; the complete C++ program below owns training, optimizer writes, checkpoints and diagnostic queries.
)
\toc

> Source experiment and explicit tensor workflow

The source uses a small synthetic moving-dot next-frame task. These are mechanism experiments with the original short budgets, rather than real-data benchmark claims. Optional TensorFlow replay is skipped when its recordings are absent. The program preserves every recorded state, input/initial-state derivative, padding/causality control and source checkpoint.

> Read the state, mask and update separately

The original48/16/16 moving-dot pools contain variable-length 2..4-step motion on a 4x4 torus. A two-channel ConvLSTM has 3x3 SAME input/recurrent kernels, followed by a 1x1 pixel head. The target is the next frame and never enters inference. Three epochs of eight-row Adam(.01) batches produce18 updates; the objective weights positive pixels by 15.

The report introduces 227 learned values in the actual source graph. Valid lengths are structural inputs, while hidden/cell tensors and weights retain live derivative paths. Inference receives inputs, lengths and explicit initial states; targets belong to the objective only.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/conv_lstm_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
